#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SessionStart hook for the delivery-superpowers method: inject the per-repo adaptations that bend
# vendored superpowers to THIS harness. Two blocks: (1) where the skills write FILE artifacts
# (specs/plans -> gitignored .local/, overriding the docs/superpowers/ default), and (2) a
# worktree-teardown correction for finishing-a-development-branch Step 6 (the native ExitWorktree
# remove-guard trips on already-integrated work). Tier-independent. Offline-safe: no network, no
# repo reads (the teardown block is static text; the agent reads .repo-visibility itself at finish).
set -euo pipefail

# Raw text -> JSON additionalContext (Claude Code hook shape). printf, not heredoc
# (bash 5.3+ heredoc hang, see obra/superpowers#571).
emit() {
  local s="$1"
  s="${s//\\/\\\\}"; s="${s//\"/\\\"}"
  s="${s//$'\n'/\\n}"; s="${s//$'\r'/\\r}"; s="${s//$'\t'/\\t}"
  printf '{\n  "hookSpecificOutput": {\n    "hookEventName": "SessionStart",\n    "additionalContext": "%s"\n  }\n}\n' "$s"
}

emit "<superpowers-artifact-locations>
For this repo, the superpowers skills write their FILE artifacts under a gitignored,
machine-local root — NOT the upstream \`docs/superpowers/\` default. Treat the paths below as the
declared 'user preference' those skills defer to:

- Design specs (brainstorming): \`.local/superpowers/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>-design.md\`
- Implementation plans (writing-plans): \`.local/superpowers/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-<feature>.md\`

Before writing the first artifact, ensure \`.local/\` is gitignored: if \`git check-ignore -q .local\`
fails, add a \`.local/\` line to \`.gitignore\` and commit it. Do NOT commit anything under \`.local/\`.

Worktrees are unaffected — keep them at superpowers' own \`.worktrees/\` default (it self-ignores
and self-cleans). Do not move worktrees under \`.local/\`.
</superpowers-artifact-locations>

<worktree-teardown-correction skill=\"finishing-a-development-branch\" step=\"6\">
Harness adaptation for the delivery-superpowers method (the worktree flow is this method's). At
worktree teardown AFTER you have integrated the work, the native ExitWorktree(action:\"remove\")
guard WILL trip: it refuses because the worktree branch still holds commit(s) not reachable from
the branch's creation-time base, and reports them as work that would be \"discarded.\" Post-
integration that wording is misleading — the commit is safely on the integration target; nothing is
at risk. Do NOT hand-run a verify-then-confirm dance each time, and do NOT reflexively pass
discard_changes:true (that WOULD lose work if integration had not actually happened).

Run ONE codified teardown: confirm integration with the tier oracle (read the tier from
.repo-visibility, as everywhere), then go straight to the force-remove. If the oracle FAILS, stop
loudly — the work is not integrated and the guard's safety net stands.

- private (in-session, at finish; push is deferred to the user, so origin/main lags):
    git merge-base --is-ancestor HEAD main      # LOCAL main; run from inside the worktree
    -> ExitWorktree(action:\"remove\", discard_changes:true)

- public (deferred until AFTER the PR lands; keep the worktree alive through review per
  finishing-a-development-branch Option 2):
    [ \"\$(gh pr view <PR#> --json state -q .state)\" = \"MERGED\" ]   # squash/rebase-proof
    -> ExitWorktree(action:\"remove\", discard_changes:true)

Why the oracles differ: a squash/rebase merge destroys commit identity, so an ancestry check is
invalid on the public path — ask the integration source of truth (gh) instead. On public the work
is safe the moment it is PUSHED (the remote PR branch holds it); the MERGED check is a \"don't tear
down until it lands\" procedural gate, not the data-loss guard.
</worktree-teardown-correction>"
exit 0
